00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.140 align:start position:0%
there is more turbulence for Boeing as  it reels from two deadly crashes of its  737 max jets in Ethiopia and Indonesia  Indonesia's national airline has  submitted paperwork seeking the  cancellation of a multi-billion dollar  order for nearly 50 737 max Jets Kris  Van Cleave says it could be just the  first wave of what some are calling a  financial tsunami for Boeing the  cancellation of the roughly five billion  dollar order is the latest fallout for  Boeing following the two 737 max crashes  that killed 346 people it presents a  huge threat to Boeing's bottom line the  737 max is estimated to account for one  third of Boeing's total revenue over the  next five years and the company has lost  14% of its stock price since the  Ethiopian air crash thirteen days ago  this is the type of thing that is a  financial tsunami if Boeing loses orders  it is billions of dollars potentially at  risk maybe tens or more but in fairness  we have only seen one airline announced  that it plans to cancel its airport's  737 max orders aviation industry analyst  Henry Hart de Velde says Boeing's  workforce could be impacted if other  airline's follow suit as with any  business when Boeing loses an order it's  going to lose revenue and so depending  on the number of orders that could get  cancelled that could have an impact not  only on Boeing's top-line revenue and  their profits but on their workers max  operator Norwegian is seeking  compensation from Boeing for having its  planes grounded the grounding is  estimated to cost airlines about 150  thousand dollars per day per plane  Boeing shelled out 500 million to  Airlines after grounding the 787 for  three months in 2013 there is no  question that this aircraft was rushed  to market lawyer Steve marks is suing  Boeing on behalf of families of 20  people killed in october's Lyon air  crash he believes the rush to catch up  to rival Airbus in 2015 led to design  mistakes in the max that turned deadly  the certification process was abused in  this case the regulator's did not do  their job  Boeing took advantage of it this weekend  American  lines pilots will test Boeing's software  fix for the 737 max for CBS this morning  Saturday Kris Van Cleave Washington
 

